<question type="stack">
  <name><text>Nature of stationary points by the second derivative</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>The curve \(y = {@f@}\) has stationary points at \(x = {@p0@}\) and \(x = {@q0@}\).</p><p>(a) Find \(\dfrac{d^2y}{dx^2}\).</p><div>\(\dfrac{d^2y}{dx^2} =\) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div><p>(b) Find the value of \(\dfrac{d^2y}{dx^2}\) at \(x = {@p0@}\).</p><div>[[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</div><p>This stationary point is a [[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt3]]</p><p>(c) Find the value of \(\dfrac{d^2y}{dx^2}\) at \(x = {@q0@}\).</p><div>[[input:ans4]] [[validation:ans4]] [[feedback:prt4]]</div><p>This stationary point is a [[input:ans5]] [[validation:ans5]] [[feedback:prt5]]</p>]]></text>
  </questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>The second derivative \(\dfrac{d^2y}{dx^2}\) is the rate at which the gradient itself is changing. At a stationary point, \(\dfrac{d^2y}{dx^2} \lt 0\) means the gradient is decreasing, so the point is a maximum point; \(\dfrac{d^2y}{dx^2} \gt 0\) means a minimum point.</p><p>(a) \(\dfrac{dy}{dx} = {@df@}\), so \(\dfrac{d^2y}{dx^2} = {@d2@}\).</p><p>(b) At \(x = {@p0@}\), \(\dfrac{d^2y}{dx^2} = {@vp@}\), which is [[if test="is(vp>0)"]]positive[[else]]negative[[/if]]: a [[if test="is(np=1)"]]maximum point[[elif test="is(np=2)"]]minimum point[[else]]point of inflection[[/if]].</p><p>(c) At \(x = {@q0@}\), \(\dfrac{d^2y}{dx^2} = {@vq@}\), which is [[if test="is(vq>0)"]]positive[[else]]negative[[/if]]: a [[if test="is(nq=1)"]]maximum point[[elif test="is(nq=2)"]]minimum point[[else]]point of inflection[[/if]].</p>]]></text>
  </generalfeedback>
  <defaultgrade>5</defaultgrade>
